This is intended to simulate an actual investigation you'd see in a SOC environment.It features use of Splunk as a SIEM, as well.
The format of this one is a bit different. There are alerts that come in and you, as the SOC analyst, have to triage and decide if they are true or false positives and why.

https://tryhackme.com/soc-sim/dashboard

The first alert is classified as Inbound Email Containing Suspicious External Link. This indicates that investigation of the link is necessary here.
This is the email content:
Hi Ms. Garcia,\n\nWelcome to TheTryDaily!\n\nAs part of your onboarding, please complete your final profile setup so we can configure your access.\n\nKindly please click the link below:\n\n<a href="https://hrconnex.thm/onboarding/15400654060/j.garcia">Set Up My Profile</a>.\n\nIf you have questions, please reach out to the HR Onboarding Team.
So we see that the potentially suspicious link is: https://hrconnex.thm/onboarding/15400654060/j.garcia

The next step is going to be to search for this link in Splunk. This is actually the only log for that link. Therefore, no link was clicked (no outbound connections were found in the SIEM). Furthermore, "hrconnex.thm" is probably legitimate, as no further details were found in the SIEM.
I'm going to classify this as false positive and fill in the details with the log from Splunk.

Next, there is another alert of the same type. Here is the content of that email:
Dear Customer,\n\nWe were unable to deliver your package due to an incomplete address.\n\nPlease confirm your shipping information by clicking the link below:\n\nhttp://bit.ly/3sHkX3da12340\n\nIf we don’t hear from you within 48 hours, your package will be returned to sender.\n\nThank you,\n\nAmazon Delivery

This one has several red flags. There is a sense of urgency, which attackers use to force you to act without thinking. There is an obfuscated (bit.ly) URL to click on, which indicates there is likely something to hide in the URL. There is also the fact that most people don't use work emails for things like Amazon. Searching for the link (http://bit.ly/3sHkX3da12340) in Splunk reveals that the link was blocked by the firewall. This means someone likely did try to click the link, indicating potential success of the phishing attempt. To be extra sure, I can search the link in VirusTotal, and it does flag as malicious. I classified this as a true positive, considering that the link was suspicious and it was likely clicked. However, it requires no further escalation as the issue was blocked at the firewall.

Next, Access to Blacklisted External URL Blocked by Firewall is alerted. It shows someone attempting to go to: http://bit.ly/3sHkX3da12340
That is the previous link that is already flagged as likely malicious. This is a true positive because the suspicious nature of the URL is already confirmed, but it was blocked, so no further action is necessary.

Finally, there is another Inbound Email Containing Suspicious External Link. This is the contents of that email:
Hi C.Allen,\n\nWe detected an unusual sign-in attempt on your Microsoft account.\n\nLocation: Lagos, Nigeria\n\nIP Address: 102.89.222.143\n\nDate: 2025-01-24 06:42\n\nIf this was not you, please secure your account immediately to avoid unauthorized access.\n\n<a href="https://m1crosoftsupport.co/login">Review Activity</a>\n\nThank you,\n\nMicrosoft Account Security Team

https://m1crosoftsupport.co/login... this URL is typo-squatted and therefore not legitimate. I checked Splunk to see if it was clicked/allowed. Unfortunately, it looks like this one was allowed. That's a true positive that DOES require escalation to remediate. The next level in the SOC will likely have to investigate the user's account and workstation to hunt for malicious activity, as well as determine if anyone else may be affected.

The simulations ends here. I got a 100% rate on true and false positives.
